# Architecture Guardrails

This project follows **Clean Architecture** in a classic layered form. The hashing
core is the domain kernel. The HTTP API and JDBC code are adapters around it. Calls
flow in one direction only:

```
controller -> service -> repository
```

A layer never calls back up. A controller never touches a repository directly, and a
repository never contains business rules.

## Clean Code

- **Names say what they do.** Prefer `append`, `lockChainHead`, `contentHash` over
  `process`, `handle`, `doWork`. A type's name is its role; do not suffix with `Impl`
  unless there are two implementations.
- **One reason to change per class.** A new capability gets a new class, not another
  method on an existing service. Split `AuditEventService` rather than grow it.
- **Small methods.** If a method needs a comment to explain a *step*, extract the step.
  Comments explain *why* a non-obvious constraint exists, never *what* the next line does.
- **No dead or speculative code.** No commented-out blocks, unused parameters, or
  types added "for later".
- **Errors fail visibly.** Do not catch and ignore. Map exceptions to HTTP only in
  `@ControllerAdvice`. Repositories throw; services translate to domain exceptions.
- **Immutability by default.** Prefer records. Defensively copy collections in the
  compact constructor rather than suppressing SpotBugs.
- **Tests name the guarantee.** `@DisplayName` states the behavior ("an edited payload
  is detected"), not the method under test.

## Module roles

- **`audit-hashing-core`** — the hashing domain. Pure Java: no Spring, no JDBC, no
  persistence annotations. The offline verifier depends on this and nothing else, so a
  recipient runs exactly the bytes the service ran. Keeping it framework-free is a
  correctness requirement, not a style preference.
- **`audit-service`** — HTTP API, persistence, verification.
- **`audit-verifier-cli`** — thin CLI over the hashing core only.

## Package layout in `audit-service`

```
com.auditlog.service
├── config/      @ConfigurationProperties, @Bean wiring
├── controller/  @RestController, @ControllerAdvice
├── dto/         request/response records used only at the HTTP boundary
├── exception/   business exceptions mapped to status codes by @ControllerAdvice
├── model/       internal domain types passed between layers
├── repository/  JdbcTemplate data access, one class per aggregate
└── service/     business rules and transaction boundaries
```

## Rules

- **Responsibility per layer.** Controllers map DTOs and HTTP status codes. Services own
  business rules and `@Transactional`. Repositories own SQL and row mapping.
- **DTOs stop at the controller.** Never accept or return a `dto/` type in a service or
  repository signature; map to `model/` at the boundary.
- **No framework leakage inward.** `HttpServletRequest` and `ResultSet` never appear
  outside `controller/` and `repository/` respectively.
- **Constructor injection only.** No field `@Autowired`.
- **Hashing lives in `audit-hashing-core`.** Never recompute a hash inline in a controller,
  service, or repository; call the core.
- **Every new hash purpose gets a new `DomainTag`.** Existing tag values are frozen.
- **Canonical payload bytes are stored as `text`, never `jsonb`.** `jsonb` reorders keys and
  normalizes values, which destroys the exact bytes the commitments were computed over.
- **Appends serialize on the chain head row.** Take `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on
  `audit_chain_head` before computing a chain hash, or concurrent writers will fork the chain.
- **Schema changes go through Flyway** in `audit-service/src/main/resources/db/migration`.
  Never edit an applied migration; add a new one.

## Quality gates

`mvn verify` must pass: unit tests, integration tests, Spotless, SpotBugs, and the JaCoCo
floor of 90% lines / 85% branches on the hashing core. Never lower a gate to make a build
pass. Run `mvn spotless:apply` before committing.

SpotBugs suppressions in `config/spotbugs-exclude.xml` must name a specific class and bug
pattern, never a pattern alone, and must carry a written justification. Prefer fixing the
code: a record holding a collection should defensively copy in its compact constructor
rather than be excluded.

Integration tests (`*IT`) need a local PostgreSQL and run against `auditlog_test` under the
`test` profile. They must not be rewritten to use an in-memory database: the append path
depends on `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` and on `timestamptz` microsecond precision.
